{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward; apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: when the market is in a downswing, the model follows too slowly and predicts rebounds that do not materialize; increase responsiveness to recent negative momentum (reduce mean-reversion strength).",
    "Overshoot in mid–late horizon: forecasts show an upward drift/plateau around days ~8–15 and again ~25–30 while actual prices continue falling; dampen forecast slope after short-lived upticks.",
    "Horizon-dependent correction: small errors in the first week, then growing overestimation in weeks 2–4; use piecewise bias correction (minimal days 1–7, moderate days 8–14, larger days 15–30).",
    "Downside tails under-modeled: during sharp selloffs the model underestimates drawdown magnitude; add an extra negative adjustment when recent 5–10 day return is strongly negative or volatility is elevated."
  ]
}
